{
  "decision": "PROMOTE",
  "why": "The aggregate latency gap is a warmup transient, not a sustained regression. Evidence from the dashboard: (1) Time series shows canary p95 decaying 210ms (0-30s) -> 130ms (30-60s) -> 95ms (60-90s) -> 94ms (90-120s), while stable p95 is flat at 93/94/95/94ms. By 60-90s the canary is at parity with stable and holds there for the final two windows, i.e. the last 60s of the test (half the run) shows no difference. (2) The headline canary numbers (p50 62ms, p95 177ms, p99 218ms vs stable 34/94/104) are percentile aggregates over the whole 120s, so they are dragged up by the first 30s; they do not describe current steady-state behaviour. (3) Correctness is clean: 0 5xx out of 2476 canary requests, 0.00% err%, and 0 5xx in every time window. (4) The per-endpoint elevation is uniform across /browse, /search and /checkout rather than concentrated in one code path, which is consistent with a process-level cold start (JIT/connection pool/cache fill) rather than a regression in specific business logic. JUDGEMENT: the warmup cost is real and will be paid again by every new pod during a full rollout, so promote via a rolling update with surge/maxUnavailable=0 and a readiness probe (ideally a pre-traffic warmup hit) so cold pods do not take live traffic; watch p95 and 5xx for the first ~5 minutes after each pod rotates. JUDGEMENT on risk balance: the only observed harm is a sub-60s latency bump with zero errors, which is recoverable, whereas rolling back a release whose steady state matches stable would block the release for no measured defect."
}
